<BaseLayout title="Fireworks for the 250th — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Independence weekend on the East Coast, celebrating the US's 250th birthday in Philadelphia and D.C., then Baltimore's harbor, a drive back up I-95, and a very long way home.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · July 2026</p>
  <h1>Fireworks for the 250th</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Independence weekend on the East Coast, celebrating the US's 250th birthday in Philadelphia and D.C.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 days · July 3 – 6, 2026</strong><br />
    Philadelphia → Washington, D.C. → Baltimore → Wilmington → Philadelphia<br />
    Pennsylvania, D.C., Maryland, Delaware and two brief visits to New Jersey · one fireworks show over the Potomac ·
    one harbor seen from 27 floors up · two cheesesteaks, one at each end · three fridge magnets, none from New Jersey<br />
    The way home: one line of thunderstorms, a six-hour delay, a missed connection, and seven states in one day
    (Maryland → Delaware → New Jersey → Pennsylvania → Illinois → Arizona → California)
  </div>

  <p>
    On July 4th, 1776, a room full of delegates in Philadelphia adopted the Declaration of Independence. On July 4th,
    2026, the country turned two hundred and fifty, and it seemed like the kind of birthday you should show up for in
    person. So a friend and I spent the long weekend on the East Coast, in the two cities that have the best claim to the
    occasion: Philadelphia, where it was signed, and Washington, where the country puts on its biggest show for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other trips on this page, the maps and times below come from the camera roll. It is a short trip
    and a short roll, a few dozen photos over four days, and whole afternoons went unphotographed. What the camera did
    record, in great detail, is two things: one night of fireworks over the Potomac, and one hour above Baltimore's
    Inner Harbor.
  </p>

  <h2>Philadelphia</h2>
  <p>
    The trip started with an overnight flight from California, landing at PHL on the morning of Friday, July 3rd. I
    arrived with a migraine, which is not the ideal way to meet a city, so the first stop was not a landmark. I picked
    up a rental car, drove to a quiet spot by a bridge, and sat there until the headache let go.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once it did, I crossed the bridge into <strong>New Jersey</strong> on a small mission. On every trip I try to
    bring home a fridge magnet (the <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">2024 road trip</a> covered most of a fridge door with
    them), and New Jersey, just across the Delaware River, looked like an easy one to collect. It was not. I came back
    to Philadelphia empty-handed, and New Jersey remains, magnetically speaking, unvisited.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rest of the afternoon was quieter: a bookstore, a chair and a laptop, which is a perfectly good way to spend a
    few hours in a city while you wait for someone. In the evening I drove to <strong>30th Street Station</strong>,
    Philadelphia's grand 1930s rail station with its rows of giant stone columns, to pick up a friend coming in by
    train. From here on, "we" is the two of us.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0703.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0703.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 1 in Philadelphia: from the airport to 30th Street Station, then to dinner in Chinatown." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: PHL, 30th Street Station, Chinatown. The bridge, New Jersey and the bookstore left no photos, so the map skips them.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was in Philadelphia's <strong>Chinatown</strong>, a compact grid of restaurants and bakeries a few blocks
    north of City Hall and the convention center.
  </p>

  <h3>Hot pot in Chinatown</h3>
  <p>
    It was hot pot, the do-it-yourself Chinese meal where a pot of broth simmers in the middle of the table and
    everyone cooks their own food in it. Ours was the classic split pot: one side a deep red Sichuan broth, slick with
    chili oil and studded with dried peppers, the other side mild. Around it, the table disappeared under small plates:
    curls of thinly sliced beef and lamb, marbled pink and white; a plate of greens; slabs of something dark and
    wobbly; and a basket of fried golden rolls for dipping. The point of hot pot is that it is slow. You drop a slice
    of meat in, wait ten seconds, fish it out, and do it again, for an hour or two. It is a meal built for
    conversation, and the heat of the broth on a July evening is part of the deal.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'philly-hotpot.jpg'} alt="A split hot pot with red spicy broth and mild broth, surrounded by plates of sliced meat, greens and fried rolls." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The night before the Fourth: hot pot in Chinatown.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Independence Day morning</h3>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0704.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0704.jpg'} alt="Map of Day 2: from Old City in Philadelphia down to the Key Bridge in Washington, D.C." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: Philadelphia, then the Potomac for the Fourth.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The morning of the Fourth was clear and very blue. From a high window on the east side of Center City, the view
    looked down over I-95, running in its trench along the Delaware waterfront, to a set of tall white concrete towers
    with gridded windows. These are the <strong>Society Hill Towers</strong>, designed by I. M. Pei and finished in
    1964, three thirty-one-story apartment blocks dropped into one of the oldest neighborhoods in the country as part
    of its mid-century renewal. Pei also designed the rows of modern brick townhouses at their feet, so that the new
    towers would sit down politely among the eighteenth-century houses around them. Beyond them, small in the haze,
    rose the glass spires of the downtown skyline. Keep Pei in mind; he comes back later in the trip.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'philly-society-hill.jpg'} alt="The white concrete Society Hill Towers and a newer glass tower above a highway, with the Center City skyline in the distance under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Society Hill Towers above I-95, with Center City behind, on the morning of the Fourth.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Old City, the neighborhood around Independence Hall, is where the Declaration was adopted and the Constitution
    written, and on a 250th Fourth of July it was the obvious place to be. We went to the
    <strong>Museum of the American Revolution</strong>, though there are no photos of it. The camera's contribution to Old City that day is not a monument but lunch. Just before noon we had a cheesesteak from Sonny's, on Market Street a few
    blocks from Independence Hall: chopped ribeye piled into a long roll with fried onions, the whole thing soaked in
    melted cheese and wrapped in printed paper that it had thoroughly soaked through. The cheesesteak is Philadelphia's
    civic sandwich, invented here in the 1930s and argued about ever since. On America's birthday, in the city where
    the country was born, it felt like the patriotic choice.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'philly-cheesesteak.jpg'} alt="A cheesesteak with chopped beef, onions and melted cheese in printed paper wrapping." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cheesesteak number one, Old City, a few blocks from Independence Hall.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Washington: the Fourth on the Key Bridge</h2>
  <p>
    By night we were in Washington, on the <strong>Francis Scott Key Bridge</strong>. It is a fitting place to spend
    the Fourth. The bridge crosses the Potomac from Georgetown to Rosslyn, Virginia, and it is named for the man who
    wrote "The Star-Spangled Banner", who lived in Georgetown near where the bridge now lands. (It is not the Key
    Bridge in Baltimore that collapsed in 2024; that was a different bridge named for the same man.) Opened in 1923,
    it is a long run of concrete arches, with a green-painted railing along a sidewalk that, on most nights, has a few
    joggers on it.
  </p>

  <h3>Waiting on the bridge</h3>
  <p>
    On this night it had thousands of people on it. Looking downstream, the view from the railing is one of the best
    in the city. On the left bank, the lights of Georgetown's waterfront step down to the river. Further along, the
    long, low white box of the <strong>Kennedy Center</strong> glows at the water's edge, and beyond that the
    <strong>Washington Monument</strong> stands up on its own, a thin pale needle on the skyline. On the right is the
    dark mass of Theodore Roosevelt Island, a wooded island in the middle of the river with no lights at all. The sky
    that night was low and heavy, a ceiling of cloud lit orange from underneath by the city, with the dark river below.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'key-bridge-night.jpg'} alt="View from the Key Bridge at night: the Georgetown waterfront and the Potomac, with the Kennedy Center and the Washington Monument in the distance under heavy clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downstream from the Key Bridge: Georgetown on the left, the Kennedy Center and the Washington Monument ahead.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The show was the big one, the National Mall fireworks, launched a couple of miles downstream, and the Key Bridge
    is one of the classic places to watch them from: high above the water, with nothing between you and the show except a river and an
    island. Everyone else knew this too. The sidewalk was packed several deep along the railing, and the far end of
    the bridge, toward the office towers of Rosslyn, was a long line of people under the streetlamps, some in
    stars-and-stripes shirts, most of them holding phones and looking downstream, waiting.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'key-bridge-crowd.jpg'} alt="Crowds lining the railing of the Key Bridge at night under streetlamps, with lit office towers in Rosslyn behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The crowd along the railing, with Rosslyn behind.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The show</h3>
  <p>
    And then it started. From the bridge, the shells rose from behind Roosevelt Island and burst above its treeline, so
    the whole show seemed to come out of the dark woods. The Kennedy Center stayed lit at the bottom left of every
    frame, and the Washington Monument stood a little above it, a fixed point while the sky changed around it. Each
    burst arrived twice: once in the air, and once a moment later on the river, stretched into long, wobbling columns of
    color on the black water. The sound came a beat behind the light, a deep thud you feel in the bridge railing more
    than hear, and then the crackle.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-01.jpg'} alt="White and gold fireworks bursting above a dark wooded island, reflected in the Potomac, with the Kennedy Center lit at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The first bursts above Roosevelt Island, seen over the bridge railing.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-02.jpg'} alt="Willow-shaped gold and white fireworks over the treeline, with reflections on the river." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gold willows over the treeline.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The colors came in waves. There was a run of pure red, big round peonies that turned the smoke pink and laid
    streaks of crimson across the water. Then came white and silver, and then a long sequence of gold: crackling
    "willows" whose sparks hang in the air and droop slowly toward the ground like the branches of the tree they're
    named after. A small boat sat in the middle of the river through all of it, a single tiny light on the water,
    directly beneath the show.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-03.jpg'} alt="Red fireworks bursting over the river, with long red reflections on the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A red wave, and its reflection.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-06.jpg'} alt="Gold willow fireworks and a red burst above the dark island, with the Washington Monument at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Gold willows, with the Washington Monument at left.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is something especially satisfying about fireworks over water. On land, half the show is lost behind
    buildings and trees; over a river, you get it twice, and the reflections soften everything, so that even the
    sharpest burst turns into a slow brushstroke on the surface. The smoke matters too. As the show went on, it built up
    in drifting banks above the island, and each new burst lit it from inside, so the clouds of smoke themselves glowed
    pink, violet and white.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-04.jpg'} alt="Violet and pink smoke lit by fireworks above the island, with green and red bursts and the Washington Monument at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The smoke turns violet.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    At one point the whole sky went purple. The smoke caught the light and held it, the river below turned the same
    color, and the green and red bursts on top looked almost like an afterthought. It is the photo of the night, and
    it was taken on a phone, leaning on a crowded railing, which says a great deal about modern phones and even more
    about how good that spot is.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-05.jpg'} alt="Multicolored fireworks bursting through purple smoke, reflected in the river in streaks of green and pink." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Green and pink through the haze.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-07.jpg'} alt="Silver and gold fireworks with a red burst near the Kennedy Center and heavy white smoke." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Silver, gold and a red glow by the Kennedy Center.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The finale did what finales do. The pace picked up until the bursts overlapped, blue on silver on gold, the smoke
    so thick that the later shells exploded inside it and lit it up like lightning inside a cloud. The very last frames
    in the camera roll are almost all blue: one great sapphire chrysanthemum hanging over the island, its reflection
    running down the river toward the bridge, and the city lights along the Georgetown bank looking suddenly very
    small and very yellow. Two hundred and fifty years, marked in the sky over the Potomac.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-08.jpg'} alt="Dense blue, silver and orange fireworks of the finale over the island, with the Kennedy Center lit below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Toward the finale.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fireworks-09.jpg'} alt="A huge blue firework above the island with its reflection on the river and the Georgetown waterfront lit at left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last blue burst, and its reflection.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After a show like that, a bridge full of people has only one way to go, which is off, slowly, all at once. The
    photos stop there, and the night ended somewhere in the Maryland suburbs.
  </p>

  <h2>Baltimore: the harbor, from the water and from the top</h2>
  <p>
    The next day, Sunday the 5th, we drove up to <strong>Baltimore</strong>, an hour northeast, arriving a little before
    one in the afternoon. The plan was the <strong>Inner Harbor</strong>, Baltimore's
    downtown waterfront, followed by the long drive north up I-95 to the airport in Philadelphia.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0705.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0705.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3: from Baltimore's Inner Harbor north through Wilmington and over the Commodore Barry Bridge to the Philadelphia airport." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: Baltimore's harbor, then north up I-95 to PHL.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The Inner Harbor is a famous comeback story. For most of its history it was a working port, lined with wharves,
    warehouses and factories. By the 1960s it was mostly derelict. The city spent the next two decades rebuilding it as
    a public waterfront, with a brick promenade running all the way around the water, a convention center, an aquarium,
    museum ships, and in 1980 the Harborplace pavilions, festival markets that cities all over the world went on to
    copy. Today it is the template for the downtown waterfront: you can walk the whole basin on brick, and you are never
    more than a few steps from the water.
  </p>

  <h3>Along the promenade</h3>
  <p>
    It was a hot, hazy summer Sunday, the kind of day when the water is the color of strong tea and the sky fades from
    blue overhead to white at the horizon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-baltimore.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-baltimore.jpg'} alt="Map of the walk around Baltimore's Inner Harbor: paddle boats, the World Trade Center, the lightship Chesapeake, the Power Plant and the USS Constellation." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>An hour around the Inner Harbor.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We started on the west side of the basin, where a dock of the harbor's
    famous paddle boats was moored: a flotilla of fiberglass dragons and sea serpents in orange, purple, green and blue,
    waiting for families to pedal them out into the harbor. Behind them rose the three masts of a tall ship and the
    glass towers of Harbor East.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-paddle-boats.jpg'} alt="Colorful dragon-shaped paddle boats moored in the Inner Harbor, with the masts of a tall ship and modern towers behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The dragon paddle boats, with a tall ship's masts behind.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-aquarium-water.jpg'} alt="Pilings in the harbor water, with the National Aquarium's glass roof and Federal Hill across the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Across the water: the National Aquarium and Federal Hill.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the edge of the promenade, the water stretches east past a line of old pilings toward the angular glass roof
    of the <strong>National Aquarium</strong>, with the green slope of Federal Hill on the far side of the basin. Behind
    us, on Pratt Street, was the downtown skyline: office towers in brick, glass and stone, one with an open white
    steel crown, another with a pale pyramid on top, all of them crowding down toward the water as if to get a better
    look.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-pratt-street.jpg'} alt="Downtown Baltimore office towers above a harborfront pavilion with pink awnings and a brick plaza." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The downtown towers along Pratt Street, from the harbor.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Top of the World</h3>
  <p>
    Then we went up. On the edge of the harbor stands the <strong>World Trade Center</strong>, a slim, pale tower
    finished in 1977 to a design by I. M. Pei's firm, the second Pei building of the weekend after the Society Hill
    Towers in Philadelphia. Its plan is a regular pentagon, which makes it the tallest pentagonal building in the
    world, and it stands right at the water's edge, on a pier, so that its point faces the harbor. Near the top, on the
    27th floor, is an observation level called the Top of the World, with glass on all sides. For about ten minutes
    the camera did nothing but turn slowly in a circle.
  </p>
  <p>
    The view north is downtown Baltimore laid out like a model: towers in every material the twentieth century could
    think of, a tower with a green copper roof poking up from among the glass, parking lots, the grid of streets
    running away toward the hills, and the long slab of the convention center in the distance. It is a city that
    looks bigger from up here than it does from the ground.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wtc-downtown.jpg'} alt="Aerial view of downtown Baltimore office towers from the World Trade Center's observation level." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Baltimore from the Top of the World.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Straight down to the west, the harbor's corner looks like a diagram. The <strong>USS Constellation</strong> sits at
    her pier, her three masts reduced to sticks from this height, beside the green-roofed Harborplace pavilions, the
    curve of the brick promenade, and a skywalk running across Pratt Street. Turn to the south and the whole basin
    opens up: the long promenade, marinas full of white boats, the tour-boat docks, and the green hump of Federal
    Hill. That hill has been a lookout over the harbor since the city was young. During the Civil War, Union troops
    fortified it and pointed their guns at the city, to keep a deeply divided Baltimore in line.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wtc-constellation.jpg'} alt="Looking down on the USS Constellation at her pier, the green-roofed Harborplace pavilions and downtown streets." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Constellation and Harborplace, straight down.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wtc-federal-hill.jpg'} alt="The Inner Harbor basin with marinas, the brick promenade and Federal Hill, seen from high above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The basin and Federal Hill, looking south.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The best view is east, along the harbor toward the open water. The National Aquarium sits on its two piers
    directly below, its glass pyramid, which holds a rooftop rainforest, looking like a cut gem from above. Moored
    alongside it is a long gray shape that turns out to be a submarine: the <strong>USS Torsk</strong>, a Second World
    War boat credited with sinking the last Japanese warships of the war, in August 1945. Further out are the white
    tented roof of the Pier Six Pavilion, a concert venue, and the little red round house of the Seven Foot Knoll
    Lighthouse, an 1850s lighthouse that once stood on stilts out in the bay and was moved here to retire.
    Beyond them, the harbor narrows and runs out past the old industrial waterfront toward the Chesapeake.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wtc-aquarium.jpg'} alt="The National Aquarium's glass pyramid and buildings on their piers, with the submarine Torsk alongside and the harbor stretching east." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The National Aquarium, the submarine Torsk alongside, and the harbor running east.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wtc-power-plant.jpg'} alt="The brick Power Plant building with the white tented roof of Pier Six Pavilion behind, and the harbor piers below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Power Plant, with Pier Six's white tents behind.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Directly northeast is the <strong>Power Plant</strong>, a big red-brick building with a tall chimney-tower,
    built around the turn of the twentieth century to generate electricity for the city's streetcars. Long since
    retired from that job, it now houses restaurants, and its tower carries a giant guitar. From the top of the World
    Trade Center you can see the whole idea of the Inner Harbor in one turn of the head: almost everything down there
    was built for ships, freight or industry, and almost all of it has been given a second life.
  </p>

  <h3>Back on the ground</h3>
  <p>
    Back down on the brick, we walked east along the promenade toward the Power Plant. It was early afternoon and
    blazing, and the promenade was nearly empty. Moored along the way is the lightship
    <strong>Chesapeake</strong>, a bright red ship with her name painted in huge white letters down the side. She was
    built in 1930 as a floating lighthouse: lightships were anchored at dangerous spots where a lighthouse couldn't be
    built, with a beacon on the mast and a crew living aboard, and she spent years marking the entrance to the
    Chesapeake Bay. That weekend she was dressed in red, white and blue bunting for the Fourth.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-promenade.jpg'} alt="The brick harbor promenade leading toward the Power Plant and its guitar tower, with the lightship's mast at right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The promenade toward the Power Plant.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-lightship.jpg'} alt="The red lightship Chesapeake moored in front of the National Aquarium, with the submarine Torsk beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lightship <em>Chesapeake</em>, in front of the aquarium.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From ground level the Power Plant is even more of a character: three stories of arched brick windows, the word
    "POWER PLANT" in stone near the top, and that guitar on the tower, which from up close turns out to be enormous.
    Walking back toward the World Trade Center, you get to see it from the bottom: a smooth five-sided shaft of pale
    stone and dark glass rising straight out of the harbor plaza, with a tangle of black steel sculpture and a bright
    "Visit Baltimore" sign at its foot.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-power-plant.jpg'} alt="The red-brick Power Plant building with a giant guitar on its tower, across a paved plaza." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Power Plant, guitar and all.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-wtc.jpg'} alt="The World Trade Center tower seen from its base, with a steel sculpture and a colorful Visit Baltimore sign." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The World Trade Center, from the bottom.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last stop was the <strong>USS Constellation</strong>, the tall ship whose masts we had been seeing all
    afternoon. Launched in 1854, she was the last all-sail warship built for the U.S. Navy, just before steam took
    over. Before the Civil War she served off the coast of Africa, hunting slave ships, and captured several. Up close
    she is all black hull and rigging, with her bowsprit reaching out over the promenade, a heavy anchor laid on the
    bricks, and her visitor center draped in patriotic bunting. On a weekend about the country's two hundred and
    fiftieth birthday, it was a good place to finish: a ship launched when the country was not yet eighty, and
    still afloat. Baltimore also came through where New Jersey had not: I left with two fridge magnets.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-constellation.jpg'} alt="The three-masted sloop-of-war USS Constellation moored at her pier, with her visitor center hung with bunting and a modern tower behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>USS Constellation (1854), dressed for the Fourth.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>North on I-95</h2>
  <p>
    On the way out of Baltimore, around two, the route passed along North Avenue, where a huge neoclassical building
    takes up the corner of a block: two temple-fronted pavilions with pediments and columns, joined by a long wing, in
    pale stone, set back behind a lawn and a wide flight of steps. It is one of those buildings that make you wonder
    what a city was expecting of itself when it built them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-north-ave.jpg'} alt="A large pale neoclassical building with two pedimented pavilions at a street corner on North Avenue in Baltimore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A neoclassical giant on North Avenue, through the windshield.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then came I-95, the great spine of the East Coast, which runs from Florida to Maine through nearly every city on
    this trip. In the middle of the afternoon it carried us into <strong>Delaware</strong> and through downtown
    Wilmington, a short sequence of brick office blocks and parking lots seen through a car window. On a 250th
    anniversary weekend, Delaware deserves a mention. On December 7th, 1787, it became the first state to ratify the
    Constitution, which is why it still calls itself the First State. It also gave me a fridge magnet.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wilmington.jpg'} alt="Large brick office buildings in downtown Wilmington, Delaware, seen through a car window across a parking lot." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Wilmington, from the car.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A little later, the road swung up onto the <strong>Commodore Barry Bridge</strong> and across the Delaware River
    into New Jersey. It is a big steel cantilever bridge, opened in 1974 between Chester, Pennsylvania, and Bridgeport,
    New Jersey, and its main span is the longest of any cantilever bridge in the country. Its namesake fits the
    weekend too: John Barry was a Philadelphia ship captain who fought in the Revolution and is often called the father
    of the American Navy. It was also my second visit to New Jersey in three days, and once again I left without a
    magnet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Philadelphia part of the trip ends the way it began, with food. That evening at PHL we had cheesesteak number
    two, from Chickie's &amp; Pete's, in a cardboard box and paper printed with Philadelphia Eagles logos: chopped steak
    and onions spilling out of a soft roll, with a fork stuck in it. It was less photogenic than the Old City one, and
    it did the job. We ate it off a seat-back tray table, already on board and sure we were about to leave. We were
    optimistic.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'phl-cheesesteak.jpg'} alt="A cheesesteak in a cardboard box with printed paper and a plastic fork, on an airplane tray table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cheesesteak number two, at PHL, on a plane that was not going anywhere yet.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Seven states in a day</h2>
  <p>
    The plan for getting back to California was simple: PHL to Chicago, change planes, and land in San Jose at 11 pm.
    Then a line of thunderstorms rolled through, and the flight sat, and sat. It finally left about six hours late,
    deep into the night, and by then the Chicago connection was long gone. The replacement went Chicago to Phoenix,
    then Phoenix to San Jose, landing at 9 the next morning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Counted from that Sunday morning in Maryland, the last day of the trip went Maryland, Delaware, New Jersey,
    Pennsylvania, Illinois, Arizona, California: seven states in one day, more than the rest of the weekend managed put
    together, and most of them seen only from a gate. It is not how I would have chosen to cross the country. But for
    a weekend about America, it was, in its way, thorough.
  </p>

  <h2>Two hundred and fifty</h2>
  <p>
    It was a short trip, four days and a few hundred miles, and on paper it was mostly food and fireworks. But the
    route turned out to be a neat little tour of the country's founding. It started in the city where the Declaration
    was adopted, and spent the Fourth on a bridge named for the man who wrote the anthem, watching fireworks over the
    capital. The next day it climbed above a harbor whose oldest ship was already sailing when the country was not yet
    eighty, then crossed the first state to ratify the Constitution and a bridge named for the father of the Navy, and
    finally, courtesy of the weather, flew back to California by way of half the country.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mostly, though, what I'll remember is the view from the Key Bridge: a river, an island, a lit-up Kennedy Center, a
    small boat holding still in the dark, and the whole sky going purple above them. A country turns two hundred and
    fifty only once. It was worth being there.
  </p>
